SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My stomach has blown up like a balloon, the cramps are unbearable, and I haven't been able to pass wind all evening.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an acute episode of intense abdominal bloating and diffuse colicky pain that commenced 6 hours ago after a large pasta meal. Intermittent functional gut symptoms have been present for months, often dismissed. Tonight, the pain is significantly sharper and more persistent. The patient is distressed, pacing the floor, and fears a bowel blockage. Denies vomiting, fevers, or localized right lower quadrant pain.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Alert, visibly uncomfortable, changing positions frequently to find relief.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is visibly distended and tympanic to percussion. Bowel sounds are hyperactive but not high-pitched or tinkling. Abdomen is diffusely tender to deep palpation, but entirely negative for guarding, rigidity, or rebound tenderness. No palpable masses. History reveals episodic bloating, mild fatigue, easily confused with IBS, positive serology (recently drawn, pending follow-up).
Environmental Observations: Tidy apartment, safe layout, access to bathroom clear.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Intermittent use of over-the-counter antispasmodics.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute dietary indiscretion flare in Coeliac Disease (Mild Atypical GI) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of bilious vomiting, lack of high-pitched "tinkling" bowel sounds, and soft abdomen without peritonitis.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of localized right iliac fossa pain (negative McBurney's point tenderness), normal temperature, and no anorexia.

Mesenteric Ischemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain proportional to exam findings (no "pain out of proportion"), normal vital signs, and lack of profound cardiovascular risk factors.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe abdominal distension and colicky pain, mimicking an acute surgical abdomen. Triage correctly mandated urgent evaluation to exclude mechanical bowel obstruction or early appendicitis. Clinical examination revealed marked tympany and diffuse tenderness but decisively excluded peritonitis, localized pathology, or obstructive bowel sounds. The findings confirmed a severe localized functional and inflammatory response (gluten exposure in underlying coeliac disease). This bedside assessment safely averted an unnecessary midnight emergency department presentation and abdominal CT scan, fulfilling the requirement for urgent, non-deferrable assessment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral hyoscine butylbromide to relieve acute smooth muscle spasm. Instructed on the use of warm compresses.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a hot water bottle to your abdomen and walk slowly around the house for 10 minutes. Gentle movement combined with heat will relax the bowel wall and promote the passage of trapped gas, providing rapid mechanical pain relief.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 3-5 days to discuss final serology and strict dietary implementation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the pain localizes sharply to the lower right side, if you develop a fever, or if you begin vomiting uncontrollably.

